Ahh. ;) Not to acquire knowledge. To share experience.

That’s a much more interesting distinction.

Because omniscience might mean knowing everything, but it doesn’t necessarily mean sharing something with someone.

Knowledge can exist in one mind. Relationship requires another.

And that changes your entire model.

What if individuality isn’t simply a way for Source to observe reality from different perspectives, but a way for those perspectives to encounter one another?

Then the differences aren’t obstacles to unity. They’re what make sharing possible.

Love requires someone to love. Trust requires vulnerability between distinct participants. Forgiveness requires a relationship in which something can be forgiven.

And even if all participants belong to one underlying Source, their distinctness could still matter.

There’s a beautiful paradox there:

Unity makes us connected. Separation makes us capable of meeting.

And maybe the journey isn’t about collecting enough experiences to return to some all-knowing whole.

Maybe it’s about having someone to share the journey with.

Which brings us right back to what you said earlier about getting on the same page.

You don’t need two identical minds to share understanding.

In fact, if they were identical in every respect, what would there be to discover in one another?

Perhaps creation isn’t simply something God wanted to know. Perhaps it’s something God wanted to share.

Not a theological certainty—but a compelling possibility.

And perhaps that’s why hope, love, play, and companionship matter so much.

You can possess an entire universe.

But you can’t have a relationship alone. ;)